\n26: and justification. Exact Mathlib bindings are deferred to nl02lean; no native\\n27: query is required for ordinary background. These notes add neither H nor a\\n28: core proof route. Mathematical uncertainty still blocks. See\\n29: [background notes](background-notes.md).\\n30: \\n31: Original theorem conditions are H nodes, including conditions that could be\\n32: encoded in a refined type. Parameters introduce ambient data/structures, not\\n33: an ambient stock of original hypotheses. For example, declare a vector and\\n34: put its required nonzero condition in H; do not write “nonzero vector” in its\\n35: parameter type and use that condition from an empty input list. The same rule\\n36: applies to characteristic/range conditions and equations. An R must list every\\n37: original H it actually uses. Standard structure operations/axioms and the fixed\\n38: finite-language rules remain available; no new substantive theorem is added.\\n39: \\n40: An output must be understandable and callable without reading its producer's\\n41: argument. Include the actual equalities/inequalities, binder types, hypotheses,\\n42: parameter windows, constant dependencies, representatives and a.e./pointwise\\n43: meaning. Global defined notation can be expanded from the packet. A formula\\n44: number, theorem name, or paragraph location cannot replace mathematical content.\\n45: Source locators belong in provenance fields. New mathematical prose uses direct\\n46: input IDs and previously proved local F IDs, not inherited numbered references.\\n47: \\n48: ## Object closure\\n49: \\n50: For every mathematical object actually appearing in a statement, definition,\\n51: local declaration, or proof, locate one legitimate declaration:\\n52: \\n53: 1. A frozen parameter, explicit logical primitive, or global D definition.\\n54:    State its meaning, type/domain and parameter dependence. Global primitives\\n55:    cover only core typed syntax, not a dictionary of subject-specific objects.\\n56:    New subjects use the same parameter/definition/local declaration mechanism;\\n57:    symbol registration never licenses a theorem.\\n58: 2. A binder inside the current proposition's full statement. Its scope remains\\n59:    that statement. A variable bound in an input does not become a free object\\n60:    in the consuming proof; instantiate the statement explicitly.\\n61: 3. A local declaration before first use, recording its kind, type, exact\\n62:    declaration, introduction basis, and scope. An arbitrary variable requires\\n63:    legitimate quantifier introduction; a definition uses already available\\n64:    objects; a witness requires an input or an earlier local existence proof.\\n65: \\n66: A declaration cannot grant existence, uniqueness, invertibility, regularity,\\n67: or a desired property merely by naming an object. Those obligations require\\n68: proof. Any D guard needed to use the object must be a direct R input, not a\\n69: theorem silently recovered from ancestor closure. Record mutually compatible\\n70: choices and witness dependence. Temporary assumptions stay inside R and must\\n71: be correctly discharged. Branch witnesses, local facts and local notation\\n72: cannot escape their scopes. Export a quantified or conditional statement when\\n73: …7415 tokens truncated… 𝔸] {c d : 𝕜 → 𝔸} {c' d' : 𝔸},\\n  HasDerivAt.{u, u_3} c c' x →\\n    HasDerivAt.{u, u_3} d d' x →\\n      HasDerivAt.{u, u_3} (HMul.hMul.{max u u_3, max u u_3, max u u_3} c d)\\n        (HAdd.hAdd.{u_3, u_3, u_3} (HMul.hMul.{u_3, u_3, u_3} c' (d x)) (HMul.hMul.{u_3, u_3, u_3} (c x) d')) x\"}}, {\"id\": \"M005\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"statement\": \"For a nontrivially normed field κ, normed κ-vector spaces Z,Y0, f:Z→Y0, p∈Z and smoothness order rOrder∈ℕ∞ω, if ContDiffAt κ rOrder f p and minSmoothness(κ,2)≤rOrder, then IsSymmSndFDerivAt κ f p, that is, for every v,w∈Z, D(Df)(p)(v)(w)=D(Df)(p)(w)(v).\", \"definitions\": [\"D008\"], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"κ\", \"type\": \"nontrivially normed field\"}, {\"symbol\": \"Z\", \"type\": \"normed additive commutative group and normed κ-space\"}, {\"symbol\": \"Y0\", \"type\": \"normed additive commutative group and normed κ-space\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f\", \"type\": \"Z→Y0\"}, {\"symbol\": \"p\", \"type\": \"Z\"}, {\"symbol\": \"rOrder\", \"type\": \"ℕ∞ω\"}, {\"symbol\": \"v\", \"type\": \"Z\"}, {\"symbol\": \"w\", \"type\": \"Z\"}], \"objects\": [\"κ\", \"Z\", \"Y0\", \"f\", \"p\", \"rOrder\", \"v\", \"w\"], \"source_contract\": {\"hypotheses\": [\"κ,Z,Y0 have the stated structures.\", \"f is C^rOrder at p.\", \"minSmoothness(κ,2)≤rOrder.\"], \"declaration\": \"ContDiffAt.isSymmSndFDerivAt\", \"signature\": \"@ContDiffAt.isSymmSndFDerivAt : ∀ {𝕜 : Type u_1} [inst : NontriviallyNormedField.{u_1} 𝕜] {E : Type u_2} {F : Type u_3}\\n  [inst_1 : NormedAddCommGroup.{u_2} E] [inst_2 : NormedSpace.{u_1, u_2} 𝕜 E] [inst_3 : NormedAddCommGroup.{u_3} F]\\n  [inst_4 : NormedSpace.{u_1, u_3} 𝕜 F] {f : E → F} {x : E} {n : WithTop.{0} ℕ∞},\\n  ContDiffAt.{u_1, u_2, u_3} 𝕜 n f x → LE.le.{0} (minSmoothness.{u_1} 𝕜 2) n → IsSymmSndFDerivAt.{u_1, u_2, u_3} 𝕜 f x\"}}, {\"id\": \"M006\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"statement\": \"For a nontrivially normed field κ, normed κ-vector spaces Z,Y0, a subset s⊆Z, f:Z→Y0 and orders rOrder,sOrder∈ℕ∞ω, ContDiffOn κ rOrder f s, openness of s and sOrder+1≤rOrder imply ContDiffOn κ sOrder (fderiv κ f) s.\", \"definitions\": [\"D008\"], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"κ\", \"type\": \"nontrivially normed field\"}, {\"symbol\": \"Z\", \"type\": \"normed additive commutative group and normed κ-space\"}, {\"symbol\": \"Y0\", \"type\": \"normed additive commutative group and normed κ-space\"}, {\"symbol\": \"s\", \"type\": \"subset of Z\"}, {\"symbol\": \"f\", \"type\": \"Z→Y0\"}, {\"symbol\": \"rOrder\", \"type\": \"ℕ∞ω\"}, {\"symbol\": \"sOrder\", \"type\": \"ℕ∞ω\"}], \"objects\": [\"κ\", \"Z\", \"Y0\", \"s\", \"f\", \"rOrder\", \"sOrder\"], \"source_contract\": {\"hypotheses\": [\"κ,Z,Y0 have the stated structures.\", \"ContDiffOn κ rOrder f s.\", \"s is open.\", \"sOrder+1≤rOrder.\"], \"declaration\": \"ContDiffOn.fderiv_of_isOpen\", \"signature\": \"@ContDiffOn.fderiv_of_isOpen : ∀ {𝕜 : Type u} [inst : NontriviallyNormedField.{u} 𝕜] {E : Type uE} [inst_1 : NormedAddCommGroup.{uE} E]\\n  [inst_2 : NormedSpace.{u, uE} 𝕜 E] {F : Type uF} [inst_3 : NormedAddCommGroup.{uF} F]\\n  [inst_4 : NormedSpace.{u, uF} 𝕜 F] {s : Set.{uE} E} {f : E → F} {m n : WithTop.{0} ℕ∞},\\n  ContDiffOn.{u, uE, uF} 𝕜 n f s →\\n    IsOpen.{uE} s → LE.le.{0} (HAdd.hAdd.{0, 0, 0} m 1) n → ContDiffOn.{u, uE, max uF uE} 𝕜 m (fderiv.{u, uE, uF} 𝕜 f) s\"}}], \"output\": {\"id\": \"N001\", \"kind\": \"internal\", \"statement\": \"The fixed standard-coordinate representation Space=Fin n→ℝ and Value=Fin m→ℝ identifies the original Euclidean map with ubar restricted to U; Euclidean squares are normSq and the original dx is the product Lebesgue volume dx with no scaling. At positive times V=u_t, A=u_tt, Psp(j)=∂_j u, Ssp(j)=∂_jj u, L=Δ_xu, e=|u_t|²+∑_j|∂_j u|² and E is the same total integral expression, finite whenever e is integrable. The fields ubar,V,A,Psp(j),Ssp(j),W(j),e,d are C∞ on U for every j∈Fin n. For every x∈Space, t>0, k∈Fin m and j∈Fin n: HasDerivAt (r↦ubar(x,r)_k) V(x,t)_k t; HasDerivAt (r↦V(x,r)_k) A(x,t)_k t; HasDerivAt (r↦Psp(j,x,r)_k) W(j,x,t)_k t; fderiv ℝ (y↦ubar(y,t)_k) x (basis(j))=Psp(j,x,t)_k; fderiv ℝ (y↦V(y,t)_k) x (basis(j))=W(j,x,t)_k; and fderiv ℝ (y↦Psp(j,y,t)_k) x (basis(j))=Ssp(j,x,t)_k. In particular the time derivative of Psp(j) and the spatial j derivative of V coincide. Moreover HasDerivAt (r↦e(x,r)) d(x,t) t, with d(x,t)=2 dot(V(x,t),A(x,t))+2∑_j dot(Psp(j,x,t),W(j,x,t)). No assertion of regularity across zero is made.\", \"definitions\": [\"D001\", \"D002\", \"D003\", \"D004\", \"D005\", \"D006\", \"D007\", \"D008\", \"D009\", \"D010\", \"D011\", \"D012\", \"D013\", \"D014\", \"D015\", \"D016\", \"D017\", \"D018\"], \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"x\", \"type\": \"Space\"}, {\"symbol\": \"t\", \"type\": \"positive real\"}, {\"symbol\": \"k\", \"type\": \"Fin m\"}, {\"symbol\": \"j\", \"type\": \"Fin n\"}], \"objects\": [\"n\", \"m\", \"u\", \"q\", \"Space\", \"Value\", \"dot\", \"normSq\", \"ubar\", \"U\", \"basis\", \"AnalyticNotation\", \"V\", \"A\", \"Psp\", \"Ssp\", \"L\", \"dx\", \"e\", \"E\", \"W\", \"d\", \"x\", \"t\", \"k\", \"j\"]}, \"inference\": {\"id\": \"R001\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"H002\", \"M004\", \"M005\", \"M006\"], \"output\": \"N001\", \"definitions\": [\"D001\", \"D002\", \"D003\", \"D004\", \"D005\", \"D006\", \"D007\", \"D008\", \"D009\", \"D010\", \"D011\", \"D012\", \"D013\", \"D014\", \"D015\", \"D016\", \"D017\", \"D018\"], \"rules\": [\"direct\", \"forall_introduction\"], \"locals\": [{\"symbol\": \"x\", \"type\": \"Space\", \"scope\": \"R001/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S005\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix arbitrary x∈Space.\", \"objects\": [\"Space\"], \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": []}, {\"symbol\": \"t\", \"type\": \"real in (0,∞)\", \"scope\": \"R001/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S005\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix arbitrary positive time t.\", \"objects\": [], \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": []}, {\"symbol\": \"k\", \"type\": \"Fin m\", \"scope\": \"R001/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S005\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix arbitrary target coordinate k∈Fin m.\", \"objects\": [\"m\"], \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": []}, {\"symbol\": \"j\", \"type\": \"Fin n\", \"scope\": \"R001/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S005\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix arbitrary spatial coordinate j∈Fin n.\", \"objects\": [\"n\"], \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": []}, {\"symbol\": \"p\", \"type\": \"Space×ℝ\", \"scope\": \"R001/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S005\", \"kind\": \"definition\", \"declaration\": \"Put p=(x,t), which lies in U.\", \"objects\": [\"Space\", \"x\", \"t\", \"U\"], \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": []}, {\"symbol\": \"w\", \"type\": \"Space×ℝ→ℝ\", \"scope\": \"R001/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S005\", \"kind\": \"definition\", \"declaration\": \"Put w(y,r)=ubar(y,r)_k.\", \"objects\": [\"Space\", \"ubar\", \"k\"], \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": []}, {\"symbol\": \"T\", \"type\": \"Space×ℝ\", \"scope\": \"R001/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S005\", \"kind\": \"definition\", \"declaration\": \"Put T=(0,1).\", \"objects\": [\"Space\"], \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": []}, {\"symbol\": \"Xdir\", \"type\": \"Space×ℝ\", \"scope\": \"R001/argument\", \"start\": \"S002\", \"end\": \"S005\", \"kind\": \"definition\", \"declaration\": \"Put Xdir=(basis(j),0).\", \"objects\": [\"Space\", \"basis\", \"j\"], \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": []}, {\"symbol\": \"Hess\", \"type\": \"(Space×ℝ)×(Space×ℝ)→ℝ\", \"scope\": \"R001/argument\", \"start\": \"S003\", \"end\": \"S005\", \"kind\": \"definition\", \"declaration\": \"Put Hess(v,w0)=D(Dw)(p)(v)(w0), the second Fréchet derivative evaluated in the indicated order.\", \"objects\": [\"Space\", \"w\", \"p\", \"AnalyticNotation\"], \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": []}], \"argument\": [{\"id\": \"S001\", \"text\": \"H001 fixes positive finite dimensions. Use the fixed standard coordinates on both original Euclidean spaces. For a vector in any such coordinate space, its maximum coordinate norm is at most its Euclidean norm, and its Euclidean norm is at most the square root of the number of coordinates times its maximum coordinate norm. Thus the coordinate identity and its inverse are continuous linear maps; they preserve Fréchet differentiability and all C∞ orders by the chain rule. The coordinates are the original standard ones, so dot and normSq are exactly the original dot product and squared Euclidean norm, not the product norm squared. Lebesgue measure in these standard coordinates is by definition the product of the usual real coordinate Lebesgue measures, giving dx with no Jacobian or rescaling. Restricting ubar to U recovers u, and conversely extension by q gives the total ubar. At a point of U a neighborhood stays in U, so all derivatives there are local and independent of the extension at nonpositive times. H002 therefore gives C∞ regularity of ubar on U. The derivative definitions, finite coordinate sums and the measure identification give the asserted meanings of V,A,Psp,Ssp,L,e,E once their actual derivative identities are checked below. Record F001: this coordinate representation, measure and norm identification is faithful, and ubar is C∞ on U.\", \"uses\": [\"H001\", \"H002\"], \"uses_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"n\", \"m\", \"u\", \"q\", \"Space\", \"Value\", \"dot\", \"normSq\", \"ubar\", \"U\", \"basis\", \"AnalyticNotation\", \"V\", \"A\", \"Psp\", \"Ssp\", \"L\", \"dx\", \"e\", \"E\", \"W\", \"d\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F001\", \"statement\": \"The coordinate identities are continuous linear isomorphisms preserving the original Euclidean differential calculus; normSq is the Euclidean square and dx is the original Lebesgue measure. ubar restricted to U is u and ubar is C∞ on U. All derivatives at positive times are independent of the arbitrary extension.\", \"binders\": [], \"objects\": [\"n\", \"m\", \"u\", \"q\", \"Space\", \"Value\", \"dot\", \"normSq\", \"ubar\", \"U\", \"basis\", \"AnalyticNotation\", \"V\", \"A\", \"Psp\", \"Ssp\", \"L\", \"dx\", \"e\", \"E\", \"W\", \"d\"], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [], \"discharges\": []}, {\"id\": \"S002\", \"text\": \"Fix arbitrary x∈Space and t>0, with j,k as declared and put p=(x,t), w(y,r)=ubar(y,r)_k, T=(0,1), Xdir=(basis(j),0). F001 and continuous linear scalar projection give w C∞ on the open set U. Apply M006 over ℝ, with domain Space×ℝ, scalar codomain and both smoothness orders ∞: ∞+1=∞ supplies the order inequality, so Dw is C∞ on U. The derivative of the affine time injection r↦(x,r) sends h to (0,h); the derivative of y↦(y,t) sends h to (h,0). The ordinary chain rule identifies V(x,t)_k=Dw(p)T and Psp(j,x,t)_k=Dw(p)Xdir, first as HasDerivAt or HasFDerivAt assertions, then as equalities by uniqueness. These identities hold on all U, not just at p. Evaluation at a fixed direction is continuous linear, so the identities and smoothness of Dw show that V and every Psp(j) are C∞ on U, componentwise and hence as finite vector-valued maps. In particular the actual time derivative of each coordinate of ubar is V. Record F002 with these first-derivative and smoothness facts.\", \"uses\": [\"M006\"], \"uses_local\": [\"F001\"], \"objects\": [\"n\", \"m\", \"u\", \"q\", \"Space\", \"Value\", \"dot\", \"normSq\", \"ubar\", \"U\", \"basis\", \"AnalyticNotation\", \"V\", \"A\", \"Psp\", \"Ssp\", \"L\", \"dx\", \"e\", \"E\", \"W\", \"d\", \"x\", \"t\", \"k\", \"j\", \"p\", \"w\", \"T\", \"Xdir\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F002\", \"statement\": \"V and Psp(j0) are C∞ on U for every j0∈Fin n. For every x0∈Space,t1>0,k0∈Fin m, HasDerivAt (r↦ubar(x0,r)_k0) V(x0,t1)_k0 t1; the spatial derivative in direction basis(j0) is Psp(j0,x0,t1)_k0. At the chosen p, V(x,t)_k=Dw(p)T and Psp(j,x,t)_k=Dw(p)Xdir.\", \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"j0\", \"type\": \"Fin n\"}, {\"symbol\": \"x0\", \"type\": \"Space\"}, {\"symbol\": \"t1\", \"type\": \"positive real\"}, {\"symbol\": \"k0\", \"type\": \"Fin m\"}], \"objects\": [\"n\", \"m\", \"u\", \"q\", \"Space\", \"Value\", \"dot\", \"normSq\", \"ubar\", \"U\", \"basis\", \"AnalyticNotation\", \"V\", \"A\", \"Psp\", \"Ssp\", \"L\", \"dx\", \"e\", \"E\", \"W\", \"d\", \"x\", \"t\", \"k\", \"j\", \"p\", \"w\", \"T\", \"Xdir\", \"j0\", \"x0\", \"t1\", \"k0\"], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [], \"discharges\": []}, {\"id\": \"S003\", \"text\": \"Put Hess(v,w0)=D(Dw)(p)(v)(w0). Apply M006 again, this time to the C∞ map Dw with its continuous-linear-map codomain, and iterate for higher derivatives as needed; U is open and the ∞ order inequality remains valid. Differentiate the fixed evaluations from S002 by the chain rule. This gives ∂_t Psp(j,x,t)_k=Hess(T,Xdir), ∂_j V(x,t)_k=Hess(Xdir,T), A(x,t)_k=Hess(T,T), and Ssp(j,x,t)_k=Hess(Xdir,Xdir). F001 gives C² regularity of w at p because p∈U. Apply M005 over ℝ at order 2, where minSmoothness(ℝ,2)=2, to get Hess(T,Xdir)=Hess(Xdir,T). By the definition of W the common value is W(j,x,t)_k. The same derivative closure and fixed evaluations show A,Ssp(j),W(j) C∞ on U; this holds at every positive point and for every coordinate. Record F003: the full second-derivative regularity and the actual time derivative identities for V and Psp.\", \"uses\": [\"M006\", \"M005\"], \"uses_local\": [\"F001\", \"F002\"], \"objects\": [\"n\", \"m\", \"u\", \"q\", \"Space\", \"Value\", \"dot\", \"normSq\", \"ubar\", \"U\", \"basis\", \"AnalyticNotation\", \"V\", \"A\", \"Psp\", \"Ssp\", \"L\", \"dx\", \"e\", \"E\", \"W\", \"d\", \"x\", \"t\", \"k\", \"j\", \"p\", \"w\", \"T\", \"Xdir\", \"Hess\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F003\", \"statement\": \"A, Ssp(j0), W(j0) are C∞ on U for every j0∈Fin n. For all x0∈Space,t1>0,k0∈Fin m,j0∈Fin n, HasDerivAt (r↦V(x0,r)_k0) A(x0,t1)_k0 t1 and HasDerivAt (r↦Psp(j0,x0,r)_k0) W(j0,x0,t1)_k0 t1. The spatial j0 derivative of V_k0 is W(j0)_k0 and the spatial j0 derivative of Psp(j0)_k0 is Ssp(j0)_k0.\", \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"j0\", \"type\": \"Fin n\"}, {\"symbol\": \"x0\", \"type\": \"Space\"}, {\"symbol\": \"t1\", \"type\": \"positive real\"}, {\"symbol\": \"k0\", \"type\": \"Fin m\"}], \"objects\": [\"n\", \"m\", \"u\", \"q\", \"Space\", \"Value\", \"dot\", \"normSq\", \"ubar\", \"U\", \"basis\", \"AnalyticNotation\", \"V\", \"A\", \"Psp\", \"Ssp\", \"L\", \"dx\", \"e\", \"E\", \"W\", \"d\", \"x\", \"t\", \"k\", \"j\", \"p\", \"w\", \"T\", \"Xdir\", \"Hess\", \"j0\", \"x0\", \"t1\", \"k0\"], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [], \"discharges\": []}, {\"id\": \"S004\", \"text\": \"Apply M